    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rockwood began in the early 1880s when Lee Shields opened a store beside Camp Creek, sparking the town’s first settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/rockwood-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened in 1889 in H. Shields’ home, and the town adopted the name Rockwood the following year in honor of local resident Adolph Wood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/rockwood-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Between 1890 and 1892, coal discoveries near the Colorado River triggered a brief boom that drew miners and businesses to Rockwood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/rockwood-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 1914, Rockwood boasted 100 residents, three general stores, and a cotton gin, though the early coal mine had already closed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/rockwood-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1926 Rockwood operated its own school, which remained open until 1955 before consolidating with nearby districts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockwood%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
